  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1150 PM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nPresent model agreement continues to hold for a trough and its\nassociated surface low to be slowly making its way out of the\nPanhandles Sunday. The exit of the trough will mostly keep any\nchance of precipitation across the Eastern Panhandles with some\npotential for showers in the north thanks to any moisture that can\nwrap around the low pressure system. While this activity may not\namount to much beyond some light rainfall, most models do agree to\nit leaving the Panhandles cool for Sunday with afternoon highs\nonly reaching into the 40s. By Monday, these cool temperatures\nwill look to translate into some chilly morning lows with most\nlocations nearing freezing that morning. Moving into the Tuesday\nand the middle of next week, present model agreement does see\nanother system try to push across just our north in Colorado.\nCurrently, this position is not very favored for the Panhandles\nwith present ensemble only giving around a 10 to 20 percent chance\nof precipitation in the Western Panhandles. Still this passage has\nthe potential to bring gusty winds during Tuesday and Wednesday\nafternoon with most model expecting a decent jet streak to follow\nalongside it. Otherwise, temperatures are expected to warm\nslightly form the weekend activity but still stay in the 50 to 60s\nfor afternoon highs.",
